From: Anthony Johnson <105193579+Johnson-Anthony@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:21:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] HID: spi-hid: end the IRQ loop after the error handler
 gives up

When the error handler runs out of attempts it powers the device down
but leaves the IRQ armed. The IRQ thread then returned IRQ_HANDLED
without reading anything, so an unpowered device holding the
level-triggered line asserted kept the oneshot IRQ firing until the next
suspend, and the storm mask never tripped because nothing counted.

Count these as errors so the existing storm mask ends the loop.
spi_hid_stop() and spi_hid_start() already balance that mask, so the
next resume brings the device back, and there is no second owner of
irq_enabled to race with spi_hid_stop().

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/hid/spi-hid/spi-hid-core.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/spi-hid/spi-hid-core.c b/drivers/hid/spi-hid/spi-hid-core.c
index cdcebf48e..466829a07 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/spi-hid/spi-hid-core.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/spi-hid/spi-hid-core.c
@@ -700,8 +700,18 @@ static irqreturn_t spi_hid_irq_thread(int irq, void *_shid)
 	unsigned int reads = 0;
 
 	do {
-		if (!shid->powered || READ_ONCE(shid->stopping))
+		if (READ_ONCE(shid->stopping))
 			break;
+		if (!shid->powered) {
+			/*
+			 * Off without a stop: the error handler gave up. The
+			 * unpowered device may hold the line asserted, so count
+			 * it and let the storm mask end the loop.
+			 */
+			if (++shid->bus_error_count >= SPI_HID_IRQ_STORM_ERRORS)
+				spi_hid_irq_storm(shid);
+			break;
+		}
 		if (!spi_hid_read_input(shid))
 			break;
 		/*
